WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.607 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
How many equal square tiles would cover a small floor?

00:00:04.257 --> 00:00:07.346 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A long piece of string cannot cover the surface.

00:00:07.566 --> 00:00:12.666 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
We need a different measure from perimeter: area tells us
how much flat space a shape covers.

00:00:13.316 --> 00:00:17.642 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Our learning goal is to explain and find area using square
units.

00:00:18.292 --> 00:00:20.043 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Success criteria.

00:00:20.263 --> 00:00:23.623 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can cover a surface without gaps or overlaps.

00:00:23.843 --> 00:00:26.885 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can use rows and columns to count squares.

00:00:27.105 --> 00:00:29.527 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
I can distinguish area from perimeter.

00:00:30.177 --> 00:00:34.050 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Arrange equal unit squares without gaps or overlaps.

00:00:34.270 --> 00:00:36.848 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each square covers one square unit.

00:00:37.068 --> 00:00:41.533 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
For a rectangle, equal rows let us count efficiently with
multiplication.

00:00:41.753 --> 00:00:47.493 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A square centimetre is a square one centimetre along each
side, not a one-centimetre length.

00:00:48.143 --> 00:00:52.988 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A rectangle has eight columns and five rows of
square-centimetre tiles.

00:00:53.208 --> 00:00:55.864 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Predict how many unit squares cover it.

00:00:56.084 --> 00:01:00.214 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Each row contains eight squares, and there are five equal
rows.

00:01:00.864 --> 00:01:03.901 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Five groups of eight make forty unit squares.

00:01:04.121 --> 00:01:07.008 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The area is forty square centimetres.

00:01:07.228 --> 00:01:13.981 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Multiplying eight centimetres by five centimetres counts the
same tiled surface, so we write the squared unit.

00:01:14.631 --> 00:01:17.776 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A play mat is seven metres by four metres.

00:01:17.996 --> 00:01:20.954 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Imagine covering it with one-metre squares.

00:01:21.174 --> 00:01:24.985 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
There are seven squares along each row, with four rows
altogether.

00:01:25.205 --> 00:01:27.473 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What would the surface measurement be?

00:01:28.123 --> 00:01:30.896 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The area is twenty-eight square metres.

00:01:31.116 --> 00:01:36.936 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
The perimeter is a different quantity: seven plus four plus
seven plus four is twenty-two metres.

00:01:37.156 --> 00:01:40.339 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Area counts the cover; perimeter measures the border.

00:01:40.989 --> 00:01:45.943 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Adding eight and five does not count the tiles covering an
eight-by-five rectangle.

00:01:46.163 --> 00:01:48.507 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
It counts only two side lengths.

00:01:48.727 --> 00:01:54.044 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
To find area, use all five rows of eight equal squares, with
no gaps or overlaps.

00:01:54.694 --> 00:01:58.845 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
A rectangle has six columns and four rows of unit squares.

00:01:59.065 --> 00:02:00.633 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
What is its area?

00:02:04.633 --> 00:02:07.886 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Four groups of six make twenty-four square units.

00:02:08.106 --> 00:02:12.171 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
You can check by counting the tiles or adding six four
times.

00:02:12.391 --> 00:02:14.738 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
No tile should be counted twice.

00:02:15.388 --> 00:02:18.763 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Area counts equal squares covering a surface.

00:02:18.983 --> 00:02:23.629 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Use rows and columns to count efficiently and always label
the square unit.

00:02:23.849 --> 00:02:27.732 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Draw two rectangles with the same area on squared paper.

00:02:27.952 --> 00:02:30.252 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Do they have to have the same perimeter?

00:02:30.902 --> 00:02:32.589 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Explain with a drawing.

00:02:32.809 --> 00:02:35.639 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Reflect on which model helped you and why.

00:02:35.859 --> 00:02:40.298 line:0% position:50% align:center size:96%
Now try the MathMagic practice, and explain your thinking to
someone.
